By ALEX HAMMER, US MEDIA CORRESPONDENT Updated: 21:19 13 Oct 2026

Whoopi Goldberg had some stern advice for Kelly Clarkson following the NBC star’s very public feud with a Brooklyn boutique this week – move on and find a new store.

Goldberg offered the counsel on Tuesday’s edition of The View.

It came as a surprise to some, considering Goldberg’s own dust-up with a Staten Island bakery in November 2024, days after the presidential election.

At the time, the cake shop, Holtermann’s Bakery in Great Kills, initially declined to take the ABC host’s business for her birthday.

Goldberg suggested it was due to her political beliefs on-air – a claim quickly shot down by Staten Island Borough President Vito Fossella in a November 2024 press conference.

He said bakers had to replace the small-business’ decades-old boiler due to a malfunction and thus couldn’t immediately commit to the actress’s order.

‘Just say you’re sorry so we can put this behind us,’ he said without naming Goldberg, an outspoken liberal who voted for Kamala Harris. She never apologized.

The entire saga appeared to slip The Color Purple star’s mind this week, as she lectured Clarkson for bashing a small-time magic and games store on social media.

‘There are reasons, I’m sure. People had a bad day and maybe misheard, misunderstood, maybe misspoke. Who knows? But it’s done. It’s done,’ she told her cohosts after five minutes of debating Tuesday.

‘If you’re not happy, then you find another groovy store. Find another place you feel is going to take care of you, because there are a lot of places you can shop.’

Goldberg claimed that she now wanted to check out the shop thanks to the publicity Clarkson provided.

Before moving on, she added: ‘I was going to say something, but I know it will get us so deep in trouble, I’m just going to say we’ll be right back.’

Clarkson’s qualms with Art of Play on Atlantic Avenue can be traced back to a Saturday X post, in which the American Idol winner claimed she wanted to buy ‘a few things’ at the eccentric curiosity shop but was rebuffed ‘as if I couldn’t afford it.’

‘Wow, dude. You’re an idiot and just lost a huge sale. But y’all should go check out Art of Play in Brooklyn if you enjoy being treated like Julia Roberts [sic] character in Pretty Woman. For sure, check it out,’ she wrote, with a thumbs-down emoji.

The post quickly went viral and received a response from Art of Play the next day. Staffers wrote on Instagram that the item Clarkson inquired about – a mechanical bird puppet permanently attached to one of the store’s walls – was not for sale.

‘Yesterday, Kelly Clarkson visited us in Brooklyn and inquired about buying ‘Molly,’ a one-of-a-kind singing and dancing bird automaton who plays an important role in the magic shows we host here. Molly is permanently attached to the wall and isn’t for sale,’ the store wrote.

‘We’re going to keep our heads down and hopefully, we can clear things up with Kelly someday.’

Clarkson responded that day, posting a message to X that included a photo of the item she was talking about. Next to it was a $15,000 price tag.

‘Cool, cool. Sure, it wasn’t for sale. That’s the narrative Art of Play is gonna go with,’ she wrote, claiming that the salesperson ‘condescendingly’ told her: ‘yes, it’s for sale but it costs 15,000 dollars so if you’d like me to find something comparable for you I can.’

Art of Play responded on Monday with purported ‘clarification.’

‘During the 5 minutes she spent in the store, [Clarkson] inquired about our mechanical bird puppet named Molly.

‘We explained that while Molly is permanently attached to the wall and serves as a de facto mascot for our shop, a nearly identical automaton is owned by the creator of the machines, a brilliant artist named Bliss Kolb who lives in Seattle.

‘He told us he would be willing to part with Molly’s twin for $15,000.’

The store said that the singer was then offered to be shown ‘a different singing bird automaton, made by a traditional clockmaking shop in Germany, that she could have taken home with her that day.’

‘It costs about $2,000. She did not stick around long enough to see it,’ staffers wrote, adding that they had reached out to Clarkson to ‘clear the air.’

On Tuesday, one of Art of Play’s owners, Adam Rubin, told the Daily Mail that Clarkson had turned an ‘enormous misunderstanding’ into a ‘reprehensible abuse of power.’

Shopkeepers are now afraid for their safety and even the store’s neighbors are being doxxed.

‘We had no idea this could happen, but here’s the thing: Kelly Clarkson did, and she chose to do it anyway,’ Rubin said.

The Daily Mail has contacted Clarkson’s rep and The View for comment.

